          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"Steps to an Ecology of Mind\"</strong><br>\nBateson's book \"Steps to an Ecology of Mind,\" a collection of his essays, became a seminal work in anthropology and psychology. It presented his ideas on mind and nature as interconnected systems, offering insights into human cognition and the environment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Lectures</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Bateson continued to lecture and write, influencing a wide range of fields. He was a scholar at the Santa Cruz campus of the University of California, where he shared his interdisciplinary approach to understanding human behavior and societal changes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bateson's Enduring Influence</strong><br>\nGregory Bateson passed away in 1980, but his interdisciplinary approach has left a lasting legacy. His work continues to be influential in areas ranging from anthropology to mental health, reflecting his belief in the interconnectedness of all aspects of life.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
